{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16\">2025 AMC 10B #16</a> and <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2025 AMC 12B #13</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_13\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 13</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Burnside&#39;s_Lemma)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Burnside's Lemma)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_13\">Problem 13</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 13\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A circle has been divided into 6 sectors of different sizes. Then 2 of the sectors are painted red, 2 painted green, and 2 painted blue so that no two neighboring sectors are painted the same color. One such coloring is shown below.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/8/e98fe7b8cda6b1001808f80cef8dc251c65b6691.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by Mathemagician108 */ unitsize(1.7cm); pair O = (0, 0); real R = 2;  pair A = (R, 0); pair B = (0.5*R, 0.866*R); pair C = (0, R); pair D = (-R, 0); pair E = (-0.954*R, -0.3*R); pair F = (-0.22*R, -0.975*R);  path sector1 = O -- A .. arc(O,A,B) -- cycle; path sector2 = O -- B .. arc(O,B,C) -- cycle; path sector3 = O -- C .. arc(O,C,D) -- cycle; path sector4 = O -- D .. arc(O,D,E) -- cycle; path sector5 = O -- E .. arc(O,E,F) -- cycle; path sector6 = O -- F .. arc(O,F,A) -- cycle;  pen r = rgb(255, 120, 120);  pen g = rgb(120, 255, 120);  pen b = rgb(120, 120, 255); filldraw(sector1, g); filldraw(sector2, r); filldraw(sector3, b); filldraw(sector4, r); filldraw(sector5, g); filldraw(sector6, b);  draw(circle(O, R));  defaultpen(fontsize(12pt)); label(&quot;green&quot;, (0.57*R, 0.32*R)); label(&quot;red&quot;, (0.16*R, 0.63*R)); label(&quot;blue&quot;, (-0.45*R, 0.45*R)); label(&quot;red&quot;, (-0.69*R, -0.1*R)); label(&quot;green&quot;, (-0.45*R, -0.5*R)); label(&quot;blue&quot;, (0.39*R, -0.49*R)); [/asy]\" width=\"325\" height=\"325\" />\n</p><p>How many different colorings are possible?\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/f/35fc3282ec84517606e29bad5f33f73b8d31d739.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}~12\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}~16\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}~18\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}~24\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}~28$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"397\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/5/5c589a4503a7c3236b551ba6caf6fa4af19aa5be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = 36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"164\" height=\"14\" /> colorings total. We complimentary count this problem. If one sectors has colors that are are adjacent, then we can do this in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d8d1f7bf72c281f742fc014d77a3e140756ef9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" />, and the remaining four colors alternate in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/8/d98e4d01fb50da2728396971777a8e5e8d0f99a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"111\" height=\"14\" /> ways, accounting for 6 total cases. If two sectors have adjacent colors, we see this degenerates to three sectors that have adjacent colors done in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/ccc2bf2138916bfe0c5962d7ccca1431c79a7639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"155\" height=\"14\" /> ways.\n</p><p>The count is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/1/42143d009218fc6ac366235faa0663fb8b4c9164.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36 - 6 - 6 = \\boxed{24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"136\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/d/69dbfc3591433d34ddef703a745cb2a37ca69828.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\textbf{OR}\\]\" width=\"28\" height=\"11\" />\n</p><p>Create an arbitrary six slice circular diagram. The first slice has 3 options, and the second has 2, third has 2, fourth has 2, fifth has 2, and the sixth has only 1 color to be chosen.\n</p><p>Understand that if \\( \\mathbb{X} \\) is the set containing all possible cases of the circle, then \\( \\mathbb{X} \\) has a correlation to another set, call it \\( \\mathbb{K} \\), which contains all the colors. This can be quite easily sought as a rotation of one element in \\( \\mathbb{X} \\) that accurately maps to another element in \\( \\mathbb{X} \\) is considered the same.\n</p><p>In simpler terms, to account for overlapping cases in \\( \\mathbb{X} \\), one must divide by 2 (as two colorings that are rotated are considered different, and we don't want that as it defeats the purpose of \"unique\").\n</p><p>Then, multiplying out we have \\( 3 \\times 2^4 = 48 \\). Dividing by 2 to account for overlap in \\( \\mathbb{X} \\), we get \\( 48/2 \\) or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/d/d6d27926cf9e8fb457c32e9ceb84893cbd02d510.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"67\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since the sectors are of different sizes, this is equivalent to ordering the colors in a line of six spaces such that the colors at either end must be different and adjacent colors must be different. WLOG let the first color be red and the second be green.\n</p><p>If the third color is also red, then two of the remaining spaces must be blue, so blue must occupy the fourth and sixth spaces. Our coloration is thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/5/2b5cc985de13ff526f4b662ad1fc1f7f5953675f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RGRBGB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>If the third color is blue, then we again consider cases. The fourth color being green forces red and then blue, so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/6/696902e7617f0951dbef00d5fb3935e4fc30d891.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RGBGRB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Otherwise, the fourth color would be red, and either of the final two space colorations work, yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/d/2ad34b90cb344fb1ebd3535e323cadf6fbfb8063.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RGBRBG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/9/5596e55881f881dddaf49feee03361cbf85d111c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RGBRGB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Since the first color can be chosen in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways and the second in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways, and there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> colorations after choosing these two colors, the total number of cases is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/1/b21853f8184e421afbcdb8ab48a8835cdd2dbaae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\cdot2\\cdot4=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"144\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are two cases on how the colors can be distributed: \n</p><p>Case 1: Every color is diametrically opposite itself. The 6 sectors form 3 diametrically opposite pairs: (1, 4), (2, 5), and (3, 6). In this case, each of the 3 colors must cover one of these pairs. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6859713adc973d3fbe97fafef9ce829757e54b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> ways to assign the 3 colors to the 3 pairs. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/6529815ff777c8354789900f8c708bfb24a6004b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3! = 6\\]\" width=\"46\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Case 2: Only 1 color is diametrically opposite itself. First, there are 3 choices for which color is opposite itself. Second, there are 3 choices for which diametrical pair (e.g., (1, 4), (2, 5), or (3, 6)) this color will cover. Without loss of generality, suppose one color covers sectors 1 and 4. The two remaining colors must then cover the remaining sectors {2, 3, 5, 6}. These must be arranged in \"crooked\" pairs, for example, {2, 6} and {3, 5}, so that neither of the remaining colors is also opposite itself. There are 2 ways to assign the two remaining colors to these two crooked pairs. This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a3a22568d5a0ba7952e1622b07c3df543d0773c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"98\" height=\"14\" /> options in this case. It is impossible to have exactly 0 or 2 diametrically opposite colors. Therefore, the total number of ways is the sum of the two cases: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/8/f58d72954167cb12f6c811288d8b4acea369736f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18 + 6 =\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"140\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Aarav_Mishra\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(casework based on red sectors)\n</p><p>Case 1) Gaps between red sectors is 2-2:\nThere are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b824aee97b45806f24a637ddf896c9a52968eafe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot 2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" /> ways to order the green and blue sectors between the reds (there is one sector of each color in each gap and two ways to order the two colors per gap). Multiply by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to account for ways to rotate the ordering.\n</p><p>Case 2) Gaps between red sectors is 1-3:\nThere are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways to assign Green and Blue for sectors between Reds (two ways to pick color for gap of size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which determines color of center sector for gap of size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> such that all other sector colors are decided). Multiply by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to account for rotation.\n</p><p>Answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/1/18107f26bf36a47a5fbb9300b11a116a50088ca9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 6 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) 24}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"182\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~Pearl2008\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Burnside.27s_Lemma.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Burnside's_Lemma)\">Solution 5 (Burnside's Lemma)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Burnside&#39;s Lemma)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We apply Burnside's lemma to this problem. Use the cyclic group <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/8/b6884719886434e3cec66b6707e7771d41125a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G=C_{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"17\" />, it has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> rotations so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/b/6eb7f6791639857450075330a6f369d6b260a1ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|C_{6}|=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"20\" />. Burnside's lemma says:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/f/dbf2d4bfe76f8c9beb0e711873b10626deb96fe6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|X/G|=\\frac{1}{|G|}\\sum_{g\\in G}|X^g|\\]\" width=\"163\" height=\"47\" />\nOur goal is to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/7/127ce7a4f082e1b97da67ecd338aed4b5a6c447e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n:=|X|=|X^{e}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> denotes the identity. Up to rotation, we can count the number of orbits. Place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/4/c7449e7a28760b8d604da75d2fdbc0e4fdd95b7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2A,2B,2C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" /> on a circle with no <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> adjacent. Start with placing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />'s, they can either have gaps of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/9/e397e23ac15b0016f6b1e293bd288d33fac2d725.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2-2$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/a/51a49e88aeb8017965edf48fa376acd59005a35a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\,\\_\\,\\_\\,A\\,\\_\\,\\_$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"68\" height=\"14\" />) or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/0/980335609f6786f368f9befb7262bd22caad9ce6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/9/f4988a5c548f0dab47b0a7dbf90ca863aa299a60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\,\\_\\,A\\,\\_\\,\\_\\,\\_$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"68\" height=\"14\" />) for the remaining places. Up to rotation, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/9/e397e23ac15b0016f6b1e293bd288d33fac2d725.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2-2$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" />, we count <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/d/a7d4eb6436649ddaf696ced1218d2a7524646b1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/0/430463718dde1d5f7f53b121e6e1491b999a6c6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\times ABCACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"16\" />, and for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/0/980335609f6786f368f9befb7262bd22caad9ce6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/6/b06a61bda35ce1ee8e0fcb3c96c1a3ce40bd7f0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABACBC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/b/42b7447597629b0cc371a8b586b619b4f005048b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACABCB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"14\" />, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/8/e2802845b5d29a0f201bb8909db9547d20d2c188.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|X/G|=3+2=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"148\" height=\"20\" /> orbits up to rotation.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> be a single cycle of the elements. By symmetry, these cases are equivalent: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/779524d9985452bd5acf8b348a15651eb5c9b5d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r\\equiv r^{-1}\\equiv r^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"102\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/9/8897b593d848d418caa9511a15f1b76aae18fbe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r^2\\equiv r^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"17\" />.  The cycle index polynomial of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/0/aa0127d0b6fc0edcf9ec62e92f34cbecc39a48ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"17\" /> is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/3/0a3919b6797739a987e1daeb4750dd012dd7beba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z\\{C_{6}\\}=x_{1}^6+2x_{6}+2x_{3}^2+x_{2}^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"235\" height=\"21\" />. \n</p><p>Counting the fixed points, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/1/461b8e6b3fa42be3b978b89d76ce27fce744c7c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[5=\\frac{1}{6}(n+2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0+3!)\\]\" width=\"212\" height=\"37\" />\nSolving, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/a/54a45f69ca359eb13aa7f8279abb60556e74311b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=5\\cdot 6-3! =\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"193\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~imosilver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>You can tackle the problem by forgetting about the different sizes at first. This gets you 4 combinations:\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/5/9e5fdb37b1f8503f822552c7e725a14e10424d55.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(300);  pair base = (0,0); real r = 1;  // Define colors pen B = blue; pen R = red; pen G = green;  // Patterns (each row = one circle) // B,R,G,B,R,G  etc. pen[][] patterns = {   {B,R,G,B,R,G},   {B,R,G,B,G,R},   {B,R,B,G,R,G},   {B,G,B,R,G,R} };  for (int c = 0; c &lt; 4; ++c) {   pair O = base + (3*c, 0); // spacing circles horizontally   draw(circle(O, r));        // outline    for (int k = 0; k &lt; 6; ++k) {     real th1 = k * 60;     real th2 = (k+1) * 60;      // Build sector path     path p = O -- arc(O, r, th1, th2) -- cycle;      // Fill according to pattern     filldraw(p, patterns[c][k], black);   } }  [/asy]\" width=\"502\" height=\"92\" />\n</p>\n<pre>B-R-G-B-R-G\nB-R-G-B-G-R\nB-R-B-G-R-G\nB-G-B-R-G-R\n</pre>\n<p>Now we can multiply by 6 since we have 6 different pieces. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/0/1b0b00b1bea021e15e3fc670bae0f8bc662074e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot 4 =\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"122\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Taha Jazaeri\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7\">Solution 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can use a similar idea to solution 6, ignoring sizes to focus on the numbers, since sector size doesn't affect adjacency. We can start by fixing sector 1 as our starting point and ordering sectors 2 to 6 counterclockwise. Next, we can do casework on one color to see the number of valid arrangements we can have for that color.\n</p><p>By fixing blue as the color for our starting point, we get 8 different permutations, as shown below:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/4/5b46bf0b617231d440e993321a0636ff92f35820.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] //Source code from sol 6 (modified) size(300);  pair base = (0,0); real r = 1;  // Define colors pen B = blue; pen R = red; pen G = green;  // Patterns (each row = one circle) // B,R,G,B,R,G  etc. pen[][] patterns = {   {B,R,G,B,R,G},   {B,G,R,B,G,R},   {B,R,G,B,G,R},   {B,G,R,B,R,G},   {B,G,R,G,B,R},   {B,R,G,R,B,G},   {B,G,B,R,G,R},   {B,R,B,G,R,G}, };   for (int c = 0; c &lt; 8; ++c) {   pair O = base + (3 * c, 0); // spacing circles horizontally   draw(circle(O, r));        // outline    for (int k = 0; k &lt; 6; ++k) {     real angle = 90 + k * 60;     real th1 = angle - 30;     real th2 = angle + 30;      // Build sector path     path p = O -- arc(O, r, th1, th2) -- cycle;      // Fill according to pattern     filldraw(p, patterns[c][k], black);   } }  //Thank you Taha [/asy]\" width=\"502\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Casework:\n</p>\n<pre>B-R-G-B-R-G\nB-G-R-B-G-R\nB-R-G-B-G-R\nB-G-R-B-R-G\nB-G-R-G-B-R\nB-R-G-R-B-G\nB-G-B-R-G-R\nB-R-B-G-R-G\n</pre>\n<p>It can be observed that the number of valid arrangements starting with one color is identical to the number starting with any other color, as it is a simple matter of changing the meaning of the symbols assigned (i.e., blue now means red).\n</p><p>Based on our previous logic, we can multiply the number of permutations we have (8) by the number of colors we can put in the starting point (3) to get our answer. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/3/533c6498ef45edb17eda80a1b486053838744d23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\cdot3 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}~24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"122\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~TheCalcGuy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-bARFv6Felw\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-bARFv6Felw</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution 2 by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfChT3CAdGA\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfChT3CAdGA</a>\n</p><p>~7.7\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B\">2025 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a r